#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
Iris setup wizard.

Interactive, stdlib-only CLI (Python 3.8+) that walks you through the two
config files you would otherwise hand-edit to stand Iris up:

    .env                                runtime configuration
    services/api/configurations.py      service list surfaced in the UI

Run from the repo root:

    ./iris-setup                 # run the wizard
    ./iris-setup --dry-run       # print what would change, write nothing
    ./iris-setup --no-color      # plain ANSI-free output (pipes / CI logs)
    ./iris-setup --help          # full option list

Screens (the wizard gates each one on your earlier answers, so a split-mode
vulnbox install only sees the prompts that apply):

    1. Deployment mode       all-in-one IDS | all-in-one IPS |
                             split-iris      | split-vulnbox
    2. Traffic source        rsync / pcap-over-ip / DUMP_PCAPS
    3. Game parameters       tick start, tick length, flag regex,
                             VM_IP, TEAM_ID
    4. Services              add/edit/remove; parses legacy helper-string
                             form in configurations.py when present
    5. Suricata              runtime dir, suricata-update opt-in, sid-tag
                             emission
    6. NFQUEUE (IPS only)    queue, iface, chains, IPv6
    7. Split-mode shipper    SSH dest, key, push interval
    8. Flag validator        none/faust/enowars/eno/itad + team id
    9. FlagID scraping       endpoint, scan, lifetime
   10. Preview + write       coloured unified diff, confirm, .bak backup

Re-runnable: the wizard parses the current .env and configurations.py first
and uses those values as every prompt's default. Press Enter to keep what you
have and only type values for fields that changed. The shipping .env.example
is the layout template, so the rendered .env keeps its section comments and
key order. Keys already in an existing .env that aren't in the template are
preserved at the bottom under a "Preserved from your previous .env" heading.

Safety: every overwrite leaves a timestamp-less .bak sibling (.env.bak /
configurations.py.bak). Ctrl-C at any prompt aborts without touching disk.
"""

